The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
if (snd_seq_write_pool_allocated(client)) {
    /* remove all existing cells */
    snd_seq_pool_mark_closing(client->pool);
}
```
Reason:
The bug occurs because the code allows the resizing of the client pool while it is in use. If one thread is writing to the pool and another thread attempts to resize it, the existing cells are deleted, leading to a use-after-free (UAF) situation where the memory is accessed after it has been freed.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
if (atomic_read(&client->pool->counter))
    return -EBUSY;
```
Reason:
This code checks if the pool is currently in use by reading the atomic counter. If the counter is greater than zero, it indicates that the pool is being accessed by another thread, and the function returns an error (-EBUSY), preventing the resizing operation and thus avoiding the potential UAF situation.

